\n\nAlso `session.adapters` is an OrderedDict keyed by prefix like \"https://\" \u2014 wait, keys are like \"https://\"? `mount(\"https://\", adapter)` \u2192 prefix.rstrip(\"/\") \u2192 \"https\" ... hmm! `prefix = prefix.rstrip(\"/\")` \u2192 \"https://\".rstrip(\"/\") \u2192 \"https:\"? No wait: \"https://\" rstrip \"/\" removes trailing slashes \u2192 \"https:/\"? Let's see: \"https://\" \u2014 rstrip(\"/\") removes all trailing \"/\" chars: \"https://\" \u2192 \"https:\" (removes the two trailing slashes). Hmm, then the key becomes \"https:\"? But get_adapter does:\n\n```python\n    def get_adapter(self, url):\n        ...\n        url = ensure_str(url)\n        # strip the scheme prefix\n        ...\n        for (prefix, adapter) in self.adapters.items():\n            if url.lower().startswith(prefix.lower()):\n                return adapter\n```\n\nHmm, and mount stores... Let me recall precisely:\n\n```python\n    def mount(self, prefix, adapter):\n        prefix = ensure_str(prefix)\n        prefix = prefix.rstrip(\"/\")\n        ...\n```\n\n\"https://\" \u2192 rstrip(\"/\") \u2192 \"https:\" \u2014 hmm that gives \"https:\"? \"https://\" characters: h,t,t,p,s,:,/,/ \u2014 rstrip(\"/\") removes trailing \"/\" \u2192 \"https:\". Then... hmm, but then get_adapter checks `url.lower().startswith(prefix.lower())` \u2014 \"https://example.com\".startswith(\"https:\") \u2192 True. OK it works either way. But actually I recall the keys being \"https://\" in some versions... In older requests:\n\n```python\n    def mount(self, prefix, adapter):\n        prefix = prefix.rstrip(\"/\") + \"/\"  # ? \n```\n\nHmm no. Whatever \u2014 I don't rely on the keys; I use get_adapter with full URLs. \u2713\n\nHmm, wait, actually there's a subtlety with get_adapter and prefix matching: if both \"http\" (from \"http://\") and \"https\" prefixes exist, \"https://example.com\" \u2014 does \"http\" prefix match \"https://...\"? \"https://example.com\".startswith(\"http\") \u2192 True